::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What factor represents 8% growth?
{
=A. 1.08#Correct. Growth uses 1 + r, so 1 + 0.08 = 1.08.
~B. 0.08#This is only the rate, not the whole growth factor.
~C. 0.92#This represents 8% decay.
~D. 1.8#This represents 80% growth, not 8%.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What factor represents 15% decay?
{
~A. 1.15#This represents 15% growth.
=B. 0.85#Correct. Decay uses 1 - r, so 1 - 0.15 = 0.85.
~C. 0.15#This is only the rate, not the remaining factor.
~D. 1.85#This does not represent 15% decay.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A population of 400 grows by 5% each year. Which model fits?
{
~A. P(t) = 400(0.95)^t#This models 5% decay.
~B. P(t) = 1.05(400)^t#This reverses the initial value and factor.
=C. P(t) = 400(1.05)^t#Correct. The initial amount is 400 and 5% growth gives factor 1.05.
~D. P(t) = 400 + 5t#This is linear growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A car worth 18000 loses 10% of its value each year. Which model fits?
{
~A. V(t) = 18000(1.10)^t#This models 10% growth.
~B. V(t) = 18000 - 10t#This subtracts 10 dollars per year, not 10%.
~C. V(t) = 0.90(18000)^t#This reverses the initial value and factor.
=D. V(t) = 18000(0.90)^t#Correct. The initial value is 18000 and 10% decay gives factor 0.90.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
In A(t)=250(1.12)^t, what does 1.12 mean?
{
=A. 12% growth per time period#Correct. The factor 1.12 means the amount keeps 100% and gains 12% each period.
~B. 12% decay per time period#A factor greater than 1 represents growth.
~C. 112% decay per time period#The amount does not decay when the factor is greater than 1.
~D. Initial amount of 1.12#The initial amount is 250.
}

